 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Imperfect Poses:** In real-world datasets, camera poses may be inaccurate, leading to noise in feature grids. Mitigated by adding TV loss [S1].
-*   **Floating Noisy Points:** Removing per-vertex deformation vectors $\Delta v$ causes floating noisy points in the reconstructed mesh, degrading lighting quality [S2].
-*   **Uniform Ray-Grid Hitting:** EgoNeRF uses a balanced feature grid to guarantee a nearly uniform ray-grid hitting rate, avoiding the need for coarse-to-fine reconstruction approaches [S1].
+*   **Imperfect Camera Poses:** In real-world datasets, camera poses may be inaccurate. This can lead to noise in the reconstructed scene. Mitigation includes optimizing camera poses jointly or applying TV loss to feature grids [S1].
+*   **Missing Deformation Vectors:** In mesh-based inverse rendering, removing per-vertex deformation vectors $\Delta v$ causes floating noisy points in the reconstructed mesh, degrading lighting quality [S2].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Image-based reconstruction optimization](6677-image-based-reconstruction-optimization.md)
-Image-based reconstruction optimization is a method that solves for object shapes, poses, textures, and lighting by minimizing image losses using gradients estimated via differentiable rendering pipelines. It sets itself apart from its siblings by explicitly formulating the inverse problem as an unconstrained optimization task where latent parameters (such as shape vectors and pose angles) are updated via gradient descent to minimize the photometric difference between a rendered image and an observed target. Unlike [Gradient-Based SE(3) Optimization](7526-gradient-based-se-3-optimization.md), which may focus on rigid body transformations, this approach often jointly optimizes complex latent shape representations and appearance parameters.
+Image-based reconstruction optimization solves for object shapes, poses, textures, and lighting by minimizing image losses using gradients estimated via differentiable rendering pipelines. It sets itself apart from its siblings by explicitly formulating the inverse problem as an unconstrained optimization task where latent parameters (such as shape vectors and pose angles) are updated via gradient descent to minimize the photometric difference between a rendered image and an observed target. Unlike [Gradient-Based SE(3) Optimization](7526-gradient-based-se-3-optimization.md), which may focus on rigid body transformations, this approach often jointly optimizes complex latent shape representations and appearance parameters.
 
 ### [Gradient-Based SE(3) Optimization](7526-gradient-based-se-3-optimization.md)
 This method refines the rigid transformation $\hat{T} \in SE(3)$ between two NeRF scenes (Scene A and Scene B) by minimizing photometric error on rays sampled from high-confidence regions. It differs from single-image alignment methods (like iNeRF) by optimizing over multiple images in the source set $C_A$ and utilizes Viewshed Fields (VF) to mask out unreliable pixels, thereby avoiding NeRF artifacts.
+
+### [Joint Optimization of Scene and Renderer](11490-joint-optimization-of-scene-and-renderer.md)
+Joint Optimization of Scene and Renderer is a photometric optimization method that simultaneously refines the geometric and appearance attributes of a scene representation alongside the parameters of a neural renderer. It solves the problem of reconstructing high-quality novel views from a sparse set of input images by treating per-pixel attributes (color, depth, normal, uncertainty, and latent features) as learnable parameters. It differs from [Image-based reconstruction optimization](6677-image-based-reconstruction-optimization.md) by explicitly optimizing the scene's underlying point-based representation attributes rather than just camera poses or static geometry, and it differs from [Gradient-Based SE(3) Optimization](7526-gradient-based-se-3-optimization.md) by focusing on per-view attribute refinement rather than global camera pose estimation.
 
